window.GENERATED_RESOURCES={"readings":[{"id":"ext-r-1","type":"reading","skill":"完整阅读 · 8题","long":true,"title":"The Evolution of Urban Green Spaces","prompt":"Urban green spaces have been integral to city planning for centuries, but their design and purpose have evolved significantly. In the 19th century, public parks were created as 'lungs of the city' to improve public health and provide respite from industrial pollution. These parks were often formal, with manicured lawns and exotic plant collections, reflecting the era's fascination with order and control over nature.\n\nBy the mid-20th century, the focus shifted towards recreational facilities, such as sports fields and playgrounds, to cater to the growing urban population's leisure needs. However, it was not until the late 20th century that the ecological and social benefits of green spaces gained widespread recognition. Research demonstrated that parks could reduce the urban heat island effect, absorb stormwater, and provide habitats for wildlife. Simultaneously, sociologists highlighted their role in fostering community cohesion and mental well-being.\n\nIn the 21st century, urban green spaces are increasingly designed as multifunctional landscapes. Cities like Singapore and Copenhagen have pioneered 'biophilic' designs that integrate nature into the built environment, such as green roofs and vertical gardens. These initiatives aim to enhance biodiversity, mitigate climate change, and improve residents' quality of life. Yet, challenges remain: rapid urbanization often leads to the loss of green spaces, and socio-economic disparities can result in unequal access to these benefits.\n\nMoreover, the COVID-19 pandemic underscored the importance of local green spaces for exercise and social interaction during lockdowns. This has accelerated the trend of 'pocket parks' and community gardens in dense urban areas. Despite these advances, experts argue that green spaces must be more than aesthetic additions; they should be planned as essential infrastructure, akin to roads and utilities, to ensure sustainable and equitable cities.","questions":"1. True/False/Not Given: The public parks created in the 19th century were primarily designed for organized sports.\n2. True/False/Not Given: Research in the late 20th century showed that urban green spaces can help reduce the urban heat island effect.\n3. True/False/Not Given: Singapore and Copenhagen are the only cities that have implemented biophilic designs.\n4. Matching: Match the following decades with the main focus of urban green spaces:\n   a. 19th century\n   b. mid-20th century\n   c. late 20th century\n   Options: i. Recreational facilities ii. Ecological and social benefits iii. Public health and respite from pollution\n5. Sentence Completion: Complete the sentence with NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage: In the 21st century, urban green spaces are designed as ______ landscapes.\n6. Multiple Choice: According to the passage, what did the COVID-19 pandemic highlight about green spaces?\n   A) Their role in reducing pollution\n   B) Their importance for local exercise and social interaction\n   C) Their ability to increase property values\n   D) Their contribution to biodiversity\n7. True/False/Not Given: The passage states that all cities have successfully implemented biophilic designs.\n8. Multiple Choice: What do experts suggest about green spaces in urban planning?\n   A) They should be purely aesthetic\n   B) They should be considered essential infrastructure\n   C) They should be replaced by roads\n   D) They are not necessary for sustainable cities","answers":"1. False - The passage states that 19th-century parks were created for public health and respite from pollution, not for organized sports.\n2. True - The passage mentions that late 20th-century research demonstrated parks could reduce the urban heat island effect.\n3. Not Given - The passage mentions Singapore and Copenhagen as examples of cities with biophilic designs but does not claim they are the only ones.\n4. a-iii (19th century: public health and respite from pollution), b-i (mid-20th century: recreational facilities), c-ii (late 20th century: ecological and social benefits).\n5. multifunctional - The passage says 'urban green spaces are increasingly designed as multifunctional landscapes.'\n6. B - The passage states that the pandemic underscored the importance of local green spaces for exercise and social interaction.\n7. False - The passage does not state that all cities have implemented biophilic designs; it only gives examples of Singapore and Copenhagen.\n8. B - Experts argue that green spaces should be planned as essential infrastructure, akin to roads and utilities."},{"id":"ext-r-2","type":"reading","skill":"完整阅读 · 8题","long":true,"title":"The Evolution of Urban Green Spaces","prompt":"Urban green spaces have been an integral part of city planning for centuries, but their design and purpose have evolved significantly. In the 19th century, public parks were created as 'lungs of the city' to improve public health and provide respite from industrial pollution. These parks, such as Central Park in New York and Hyde Park in London, were designed with large open lawns, tree-lined paths, and recreational facilities. They were intended to be accessible to all citizens, although in practice, some were exclusive to certain social classes.\n\nBy the mid-20th century, the focus shifted towards functional green spaces, such as community gardens and playgrounds, particularly in post-war housing estates. These spaces were often small and utilitarian, designed to serve immediate local needs rather than provide aesthetic or health benefits. However, research in the 1980s began to highlight the psychological and social advantages of green spaces, leading to a renewed interest in their design.\n\nIn recent decades, urban green spaces have been reimagined as multifunctional landscapes that address environmental challenges. Green roofs, rain gardens, and pocket parks are now common features in many cities, designed to manage stormwater, reduce the urban heat island effect, and promote biodiversity. For instance, Singapore's 'City in a Garden' initiative has integrated greenery into high-rise buildings, while cities like Copenhagen have developed 'climate parks' that double as flood defenses.\n\nDespite these innovations, challenges remain. Gentrification often follows the development of new green spaces, displacing low-income residents. Additionally, maintenance costs can be high, and some projects fail to deliver promised benefits due to poor planning or lack of community engagement. Nevertheless, the COVID-19 pandemic underscored the importance of accessible green spaces for mental and physical well-being, prompting many cities to invest further in their expansion and improvement.","questions":["1. In the 19th century, public parks were primarily created to address industrial pollution. (True/False/Not Given)","2. Hyde Park in London was mentioned as an example of a 19th-century park. (True/False/Not Given)","3. Community gardens in the mid-20th century were designed to be aesthetically pleasing. (True/False/Not Given)","4. Match the following descriptions with the correct time period: A. 19th century, B. mid-20th century, C. recent decades. Write the correct letter A, B, or C next to each statement.\n   i. Green spaces were designed to manage stormwater.\n   ii. Parks were intended to be accessible to all citizens.\n   iii. Green spaces were often small and utilitarian.","5. Complete the sentence: Singapore's 'City in a Garden' initiative has integrated greenery into ______.","6. According to the passage, what is one challenge associated with new green spaces? (Multiple Choice)\n   A. They are too expensive to build.\n   B. They often lead to gentrification.\n   C. They are not accessible to the public.\n   D. They fail to reduce urban heat.","7. Complete the sentence: The COVID-19 pandemic highlighted the importance of green spaces for ______.","8. What does the passage say about the maintenance costs of green spaces? (True/False/Not Given)"],"answers":["1. True. The passage states that in the 19th century, public parks were created as 'lungs of the city' to improve public health and provide respite from industrial pollution.","2. True. Hyde Park in London is explicitly mentioned as an example of a 19th-century park.","3. False. The passage says that mid-20th century green spaces were 'often small and utilitarian, designed to serve immediate local needs rather than provide aesthetic or health benefits.'","4. i. C (recent decades) - 'Green roofs, rain gardens, and pocket parks are now common features... designed to manage stormwater'. ii. A (19th century) - 'They were intended to be accessible to all citizens'. iii. B (mid-20th century) - 'These spaces were often small and utilitarian'.","5. high-rise buildings. The passage states: 'Singapore's 'City in a Garden' initiative has integrated greenery into high-rise buildings'.","6. B. The passage says: 'Gentrification often follows the development of new green spaces, displacing low-income residents.'","7. mental and physical well-being. The passage says: 'the COVID-19 pandemic underscored the importance of accessible green spaces for mental and physical well-being'.","8. True. The passage states: 'Additionally, maintenance costs can be high'."]},{"id":"ext-r-3","type":"reading","skill":"完整阅读 · 8题","long":true,"title":"The Evolution of Urban Green Spaces","prompt":"Urban green spaces have been an integral part of city planning for centuries, but their design and purpose have evolved significantly. In the 19th century, public parks were created as 'lungs of the city' to improve air quality and provide respite from industrial pollution. These parks were often formal, with manicured lawns and strict rules of conduct. By the mid-20th century, the focus shifted to recreational facilities, such as playgrounds and sports fields, to promote physical activity and community cohesion.\n\nIn recent decades, the role of urban green spaces has expanded to address environmental and social challenges. They are now recognized for their ability to mitigate the urban heat island effect, manage stormwater, and support biodiversity. For instance, the High Line in New York City, a former elevated railway transformed into a linear park, demonstrates how green spaces can revitalize neglected areas and boost local economies. Similarly, Singapore's Gardens by the Bay integrates sustainable technologies, such as solar panels and rainwater harvesting, to create a self-sufficient ecosystem.\n\nHowever, the creation and maintenance of these spaces are not without controversy. Critics argue that gentrification often follows, displacing low-income residents who cannot afford rising property values. Others point out that not all green spaces are equally accessible; marginalized communities may have limited access to well-maintained parks. To address these issues, some cities have adopted participatory design processes, involving residents in planning to ensure that green spaces meet diverse needs.\n\nDespite challenges, the trend towards multifunctional green spaces continues. They are increasingly seen as essential infrastructure for sustainable cities, offering health, environmental, and social benefits. As urbanization accelerates, the demand for such spaces is likely to grow, making their equitable distribution a critical urban policy issue.","questions":"1. The public parks created in the 19th century were designed primarily for recreational activities like sports.\n2. The High Line in New York City was originally built as a park.\n3. According to the passage, all urban green spaces are equally accessible to all communities.\n4. Match the following decades with the main focus of urban green spaces:\n   A. 19th century\n   B. mid-20th century\n   C. recent decades\n   Options: i. environmental and social challenges, ii. recreational facilities, iii. improving air quality and providing respite.\n5. Complete the sentence: Singapore's Gardens by the Bay uses sustainable technologies such as solar panels and ______ to create a self-sufficient ecosystem.\n6. What is one criticism of creating new urban green spaces?\n   A. They are too expensive to maintain.\n   B. They can lead to gentrification and displacement.\n   C. They attract too many tourists.\n   D. They reduce biodiversity.\n7. According to the passage, what is a potential solution to ensure green spaces meet diverse needs?\n8. The passage suggests that the demand for urban green spaces will decrease as urbanization accelerates.","answers":"1. False - The passage states that 19th-century parks were created to improve air quality and provide respite from industrial pollution, not primarily for recreational activities; recreational focus came in the mid-20th century.\n2. Not Given - The passage mentions that the High Line is a former elevated railway transformed into a park, but it does not state what it was originally built as; it only says it was a railway, not that it was built as a park.\n3. False - The passage says that not all green spaces are equally accessible, and marginalized communities may have limited access.\n4. A-iii, B-ii, C-i - The 19th century focused on improving air quality and respite; mid-20th century on recreational facilities; recent decades on environmental and social challenges.\n5. rainwater harvesting - The passage mentions 'solar panels and rainwater harvesting'.\n6. B - The passage states that critics argue gentrification often follows, displacing low-income residents.\n7. Participatory design processes - The passage says some cities have adopted participatory design processes, involving residents in planning.\n8. False - The passage says the demand is likely to grow as urbanization accelerates, not decrease."},{"id":"ext-r-4","type":"reading","skill":"完整阅读 · 8题","long":true,"title":"The Rise of Vertical Farming","prompt":"Vertical farming is an innovative agricultural practice that involves growing crops in stacked layers, often in controlled indoor environments. This method aims to maximise land use and reduce the distance food travels to consumers. By cultivating plants in vertically inclined surfaces or integrated structures such as skyscrapers, vertical farms can produce food in urban areas where land is scarce. The concept was first proposed in the early 20th century, but it gained significant attention in the 1990s with advancements in hydroponics, aeroponics, and LED lighting. \n\nOne of the primary advantages of vertical farming is its ability to produce crops year-round, unaffected by weather conditions. This is achieved by maintaining optimal temperature, humidity, and light levels. As a result, yields can be significantly higher than traditional farming. For example, a vertical farm can produce up to 10 times more lettuce per square meter than a conventional farm. Additionally, vertical farming uses up to 95% less water, as water is recirculated in closed-loop systems. \n\nHowever, vertical farming is not without challenges. The initial setup costs are high, primarily due to the technology required for lighting and climate control. Energy consumption is also a major concern; artificial lighting and HVAC systems can consume large amounts of electricity, contributing to carbon emissions if the energy source is not renewable. Furthermore, only a limited range of crops, such as leafy greens and herbs, are currently economically viable to grow vertically. Staple crops like wheat and rice are not yet feasible due to their size and light requirements. \n\nDespite these challenges, proponents argue that vertical farming can complement traditional agriculture, especially in regions with extreme climates or limited arable land. As technology improves and renewable energy becomes more accessible, vertical farming could play a crucial role in sustainable food production for a growing global population.","questions":"1. Vertical farming was first proposed in the 1990s. (True/False/Not Given)\n2. Vertical farming can produce higher yields per square meter than traditional farming for some crops. (True/False/Not Given)\n3. Vertical farming uses less water than traditional farming. (True/False/Not Given)\n4. Match the following statements with the correct paragraph (A, B, C, or D).\n   i. The benefits of vertical farming in terms of yield and water usage. (Paragraph ___)\n   ii. The challenges faced by vertical farming. (Paragraph ___)\n   iii. The potential role of vertical farming in the future. (Paragraph ___)\n   iv. The definition and early history of vertical farming. (Paragraph ___)\n5. Complete the sentence with NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage: Vertical farming can produce crops year-round because it is unaffected by ________.\n6. Complete the sentence with NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage: The high energy consumption of vertical farming is mainly due to artificial lighting and ________ systems.\n7. Multiple choice: What is one reason why staple crops like wheat and rice are not commonly grown in vertical farms?\n   A) They require too much water.\n   B) They are not economically viable due to their size and light requirements.\n   C) They cannot be grown hydroponically.\n   D) They take too long to grow.\n8. Multiple choice: According to the passage, what could make vertical farming more sustainable in the future?\n   A) Reducing the initial setup costs.\n   B) Using renewable energy sources.\n   C) Growing more types of crops.\n   D) Expanding to rural areas.","answers":"1. False - The passage states that the concept was first proposed in the early 20th century, not the 1990s.\n2. True - The passage mentions that yields can be significantly higher, with lettuce producing up to 10 times more per square meter.\n3. True - The passage says vertical farming uses up to 95% less water.\n4. i. B; ii. C; iii. D; iv. A - Paragraph A defines vertical farming and its history; B discusses benefits; C covers challenges; D talks about future potential.\n5. weather conditions - The passage says 'unaffected by weather conditions'.\n6. HVAC - The passage mentions 'HVAC systems' as a major energy consumer.\n7. B - The passage states that staple crops are not yet feasible due to their size and light requirements.\n8. B - The passage suggests that as renewable energy becomes more accessible, vertical farming could be more sustainable."},{"id":"ext-r-5","type":"reading","skill":"完整阅读 · 8题","long":true,"title":"The Rise of Vertical Farming","prompt":"Vertical farming is a method of growing crops in stacked layers, often in controlled indoor environments. This approach has gained popularity in recent years as a potential solution to food security and sustainability challenges. By growing plants vertically, farmers can produce more food per square meter of land, using less water and no pesticides. However, the energy requirements for artificial lighting and climate control are significant, raising questions about the overall environmental benefits. Some critics argue that vertical farming is only viable for high-value, perishable crops like leafy greens and herbs, and that it cannot replace traditional agriculture for staple crops such as wheat and rice. Nevertheless, ongoing research aims to improve efficiency and expand the range of crops that can be grown vertically.","questions":"1. Vertical farming involves growing crops in stacked layers.\n2. Vertical farming uses more water than traditional farming.\n3. Vertical farming is currently used for staple crops like wheat and rice.\n4. The energy requirements of vertical farming are a concern.\n5. Match the following statement with the correct crop type: 'Vertical farming is most suitable for ...'\nA. wheat and rice\nB. leafy greens and herbs\nC. root vegetables\n6. Complete the sentence: 'Vertical farming can produce more food per square meter because ...'\n7. What is one benefit of vertical farming mentioned in the passage?\nA. It uses less land.\nB. It requires no energy.\nC. It is cheaper than traditional farming.\n8. According to the passage, what is a criticism of vertical farming?","answers":"1. True - The passage states that vertical farming involves growing crops in stacked layers.\n2. False - The passage says it uses less water, not more.\n3. False - The passage states that it cannot replace traditional agriculture for staple crops like wheat and rice, implying it is not currently used for them.\n4. True - The passage mentions that energy requirements are significant and raise questions.\n5. B - The passage says it is viable for high-value, perishable crops like leafy greens and herbs.\n6. '... it uses stacked layers, allowing more production per square meter.' - The passage explains that growing vertically increases food production per square meter.\n7. A - The passage states that farmers can produce more food per square meter, implying less land is needed.\n8. It is only viable for high-value crops and has significant energy requirements. - The passage mentions both points as criticisms."}],"listenings":[{"id":"ext-l-1","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":4,"title":"University Orientation: Settling into Campus Life","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and a very warm welcome to the University of Wessex. I'm delighted to see so many new faces here today. My name is Dr. Eleanor Hastings, and I'm the Dean of Student Affairs. This orientation session is designed to help you settle into campus life smoothly, so please listen carefully as I run through some essential information. First, let's talk about accommodation. Most of you will be staying in our halls of residence, which are located on the north and south sides of the campus. The north halls, including Bramble and Hawthorn, are just a five-minute walk from the main lecture theatres, while the south halls, like Willow and Oak, are closer to the sports centre. If you have any issues with your room, such as a broken heater or a noisy neighbour, please report them to the Residential Support Office, which is open from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. on weekdays. For emergencies outside these hours, contact the night porter on duty. Now, regarding academic matters, your induction week starts next Monday. You'll receive your timetable via email by Friday evening. It's crucial to check your student portal regularly, as room changes and additional seminars will be posted there. The main library is open 24/7 during term time, but you'll need your student ID card to enter after 10 p.m. If you lose your card, visit the Student Services Hub on the ground floor of the Atrium Building. They can issue a replacement for a small fee of £10. Let's move on to health and wellbeing. The campus health centre is situated next to the pharmacy in the Central Square. You can register with a doctor there by bringing your passport and proof of address. Counselling services are also available free of charge; just drop in or book an appointment online. Finally, a word about social activities. The Students' Union organises a Freshers' Fair this Wednesday from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. in the Great Hall. Over 150 clubs and societies will be there, from debating to dragon boating. It's a fantastic opportunity to make friends and try something new. Remember, your first few weeks can feel overwhelming, but support is always available. Don't hesitate to ask for help. Thank you for listening, and I wish you all a wonderful start to your university journey.","questions":"1. Where are the north halls of residence located in relation to the main lecture theatres?\n2. What should students do if they have a problem with their room during weekdays?\n3. When will students receive their timetable for induction week?\n4. What is required to enter the main library after 10 p.m.?\n5. How much does it cost to replace a lost student ID card?\n6. Where is the campus health centre located?\n7. What documents are needed to register with a doctor at the health centre?\n8. What event is taking place on Wednesday from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. in the Great Hall?","answers":"1. A five-minute walk away (the north halls are just a five-minute walk from the main lecture theatres).\n2. Report it to the Residential Support Office (which is open from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. on weekdays).\n3. By Friday evening (the timetable will be sent via email by Friday evening).\n4. A student ID card (you need your student ID card to enter after 10 p.m.).\n5. £10 (a replacement costs a small fee of £10).\n6. Next to the pharmacy in Central Square (the health centre is situated next to the pharmacy).\n7. Passport and proof of address (bring these to register with a doctor).\n8. The Freshers' Fair (organised by the Students' Union).","audio":"audio/listening-4.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-2","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":5,"title":"University Orientation: A Guide to Campus Life","prompt":"Welcome to the University of Northampton's orientation session. I'm Dr. Eleanor Hughes, Dean of Students, and I'm delighted to welcome you all to our campus. Today, I'll be giving you an overview of the key services and facilities available to support you during your studies. First, let's talk about the library. The main library is open 24/7 during term time, but please note that from Monday to Friday, between 9 am and 5 pm, you can get help from the librarians at the information desk on the ground floor. Outside these hours, you can use the self-service kiosks. For subject-specific support, each department has a dedicated librarian. For example, if you're studying History, your librarian is based in the Reading Room on the second floor of the library. Now, about IT services: all students are automatically given a university email account and access to the online learning platform, 'StudyNet'. If you encounter any technical issues, the IT Helpdesk is located in the Learning Hub, Room 1.15. They're open from 8 am to 8 pm on weekdays and 10 am to 4 pm on weekends. You can also call them on extension 2233. Next, let's discuss health and wellbeing. The campus health centre is in the Student Services Building, next to the coffee shop. You need to register with them as soon as possible, ideally within your first week. They offer general medical advice, vaccinations, and mental health support. Appointments can be booked online or by phone. For urgent matters outside opening hours, please contact the NHS helpline. Moving on to sports facilities: the sports centre is at the far end of campus, behind the student accommodation. It has a gym, swimming pool, and courts for badminton and basketball. Membership costs £120 per year, or £15 per month. If you're interested in joining a sports club, there's a fair next Wednesday in the Students' Union from 10 am to 3 pm. Finally, a quick note on safety: the campus security office is open 24 hours a day, and you can reach them on extension 2222. In case of emergency, always call 999 first. Remember, your student ID card gives you access to all buildings after hours, so carry it with you at all times. That concludes the overview. If you have any questions, feel free to ask now or visit our website. Thank you.","questions":"1. What are the library's staffed hours on weekdays? \n2. Where is the librarian for History students based? \n3. What is the name of the online learning platform? \n4. Where is the IT Helpdesk located? \n5. What should students do within their first week regarding health services? \n6. How much does annual sports centre membership cost? \n7. When and where is the sports club fair? \n8. What number should you call in an emergency?","answers":"1. 9 am to 5 pm, Monday to Friday. The speaker says the information desk is staffed during these hours. \n2. In the Reading Room on the second floor of the library. The speaker mentions this as an example. \n3. StudyNet. The speaker states it is the online learning platform. \n4. In the Learning Hub, Room 1.15. The speaker gives this location. \n5. Register with the campus health centre. The speaker advises doing this ideally within the first week. \n6. £120 per year. The speaker mentions this cost. \n7. Next Wednesday, from 10 am to 3 pm, in the Students' Union. The speaker provides these details. \n8. 999. The speaker says to always call 999 first in an emergency.","audio":"audio/listening-5.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-3","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":6,"title":"University Orientation: Campus Services and Academic Support","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the University of Wessex. I'm Dr. Eleanor Hughes, and I'm here to give you a brief overview of the campus services and academic support available to you. This orientation is designed to help you settle in quickly and make the most of your time here. First, let's talk about the library. The main library is open from 8 a.m. to midnight on weekdays, and from 10 a.m. to 8 p.m. on weekends. You can borrow up to 15 books at a time, and the loan period is three weeks. There are also group study rooms that you can book online. Now, about academic support: every student is assigned a personal tutor. You should meet your tutor at least once a term, but you can also email them if you need advice. The Student Support Centre is located in the Old Building, on the ground floor. They offer free workshops on essay writing, time management, and exam preparation. I highly recommend attending at least one workshop in your first term. For health and wellbeing, the campus health centre is next to the sports hall. You need to register with them as soon as possible. If you have any dietary requirements, the canteen in the Student Union building can accommodate you—just speak to the catering manager. Finally, a word about IT services: the IT helpdesk is on the first floor of the library. They can help with Wi-Fi, printing, and software. Wi-Fi is free across campus; just log in with your student ID. Remember, all these services are included in your tuition fees, so please use them. If you have any questions, feel free to ask me after this session. Thank you for listening, and I wish you all a successful year ahead.","questions":["1. What are the library's opening hours on weekdays?","2. How many books can a student borrow at one time?","3. What is the loan period for books?","4. How often should students meet their personal tutor?","5. Where is the Student Support Centre located?","6. What workshops does the Student Support Centre offer? (Name two)","7. Where is the campus health centre?","8. Where is the IT helpdesk located?"],"answers":["1. 8 a.m. to midnight.","2. 15 books.","3. Three weeks.","4. At least once a term.","5. In the Old Building, on the ground floor.","6. Essay writing and time management (also exam preparation).","7. Next to the sports hall.","8. On the first floor of the library."],"audio":"audio/listening-6.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-4","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":7,"title":"University Orientation: Welcome to Northgate University","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Northgate University. I'm Dr. Eleanor Hughes, your Vice-Chancellor for Student Experience. I'm delighted to see so many new faces here today. This orientation session will cover essential information to help you settle in. First, let me give you a brief overview of the campus. Northgate has three main sites: the Riverside Campus, where most of you will have your lectures; the Hilltop Campus, home to the science and engineering departments; and the City Centre Campus, which houses the business school and our new library. A free shuttle bus runs between all three sites every fifteen minutes from 7 am to 10 pm. You'll need your student ID card to board. Now, about your ID cards: if you haven't collected yours yet, please go to the Student Services Centre in the Riverside Building. It's open from 9 am to 5 pm, Monday to Friday. You'll need a passport-sized photo and proof of enrolment. Next, I'd like to draw your attention to the Students' Union. They organise over 150 clubs and societies, from debating to dragon boating. Freshers' Fair takes place next Wednesday in the Great Hall from 10 am to 4 pm. It's a fantastic opportunity to sign up and meet new people. Now, a few words about academic support. The Writing Centre offers one-to-one consultations and workshops. You can book sessions online through the student portal. Also, the Maths Support Hub provides drop-in help for anyone struggling with quantitative courses. Both services are free. Finally, let me remind you about health and safety. In an emergency, dial 3333 from any campus phone or 0123 456 7890 from your mobile. First aid kits are located in every building's reception. If you have any questions, feel free to ask the student ambassadors in blue shirts. Enjoy your time at Northgate!","questions":"1. What is the name of the university? 2. How often does the free shuttle bus run between campuses? 3. Where can students collect their ID cards? 4. What proof is needed to collect an ID card? 5. When is the Freshers' Fair? 6. Where is the Freshers' Fair held? 7. How can students book a session at the Writing Centre? 8. What number should be dialled from a mobile in an emergency?","answers":"1. Northgate University. 2. Every fifteen minutes. 3. At the Student Services Centre in the Riverside Building. 4. A passport-sized photo and proof of enrolment. 5. Next Wednesday. 6. In the Great Hall. 7. Online through the student portal. 8. 0123 456 7890.","audio":"audio/listening-7.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-5","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":8,"title":"University Orientation: A Guide to Campus Services","prompt":"Welcome to Northfield University. I'm Sarah, your orientation officer, and I'm delighted to introduce you to the range of services we offer to support your academic journey. First, let's talk about the library. The main library is open from 8 a.m. to 10 p.m. on weekdays, and from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. on weekends. You can borrow up to 15 books at a time, and the loan period is three weeks. If you need help with research, you can book a session with a subject librarian, who can guide you through our extensive online databases. Next, the student health centre. It's located on the ground floor of the Watson Building, next to the campus pharmacy. Appointments can be made online or by phone, and walk-in clinics are available every morning from 9 to 11. Don't forget to register with the centre as soon as possible, so you can access free consultations and vaccinations. For those interested in sports, the sports centre offers a wide range of facilities, including a swimming pool, a gym, and indoor courts. Membership costs 120 pounds per year, but you can also pay 15 pounds for a day pass. There are over 50 sports clubs, from rowing to archery, and you can join any of them during the freshers' fair. Now, about academic support. The study skills team runs workshops on essay writing, time management, and exam preparation. These are free and take place every Wednesday afternoon in the Learning Resource Centre. If you need one-to-one help, you can email studyhelp at northfield dot ac dot uk. Finally, a word on careers. The careers service is in the Student Hub, and they offer CV checks, mock interviews, and job fairs throughout the year. I highly recommend visiting them in your first term to start planning your future. That's all for now. Enjoy your time at Northfield!","questions":"1. What are the weekend opening hours of the main library? 2. How many books can students borrow at one time? 3. Where is the student health centre located? 4. When are walk-in clinics available at the health centre? 5. How much does an annual membership at the sports centre cost? 6. Where do the study skills workshops take place? 7. What is the email address for one-to-one study help? 8. What services does the careers service offer?","answers":"1. 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. (The speaker says 'from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. on weekends.') 2. 15 books (The speaker says 'You can borrow up to 15 books at a time.') 3. On the ground floor of the Watson Building (The speaker says 'It's located on the ground floor of the Watson Building.') 4. Every morning from 9 to 11 (The speaker says 'walk-in clinics are available every morning from 9 to 11.') 5. 120 pounds (The speaker says 'Membership costs 120 pounds per year.') 6. In the Learning Resource Centre (The speaker says 'take place every Wednesday afternoon in the Learning Resource Centre.') 7. studyhelp@northfield.ac.uk (The speaker says 'email studyhelp at northfield dot ac dot uk.') 8. CV checks, mock interviews, and job fairs (The speaker says 'they offer CV checks, mock interviews, and job fairs throughout the year.')","audio":"audio/listening-8.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-6","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":9,"title":"Welcome to Northbridge University: International Student Orientation","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Northbridge University. My name is Eleanor Whitfield, and I'm the International Student Adviser here. I'm delighted to see so many of you at this orientation session, and I hope you're settling in well. Today I want to run through a few practical matters that will help you during your first weeks. First, your student identification card. You should have collected it during registration, but if you haven't, please visit the Student Services desk on the ground floor of the Hartley Building. The desk is open from nine to five on weekdays, and until midday on Saturdays. You'll need your passport and your offer letter. Please carry your card with you at all times, as you'll need it to enter the library, the sports centre, and some lecture theatres. Second, healthcare. If you're studying for more than six months, you're entitled to free treatment under the National Health Service. You must register with a local doctor, known here as a GP. The nearest surgery to campus is the Mill Road Practice, just a ten-minute walk from the main gate. Register as soon as possible, because it can take a week or two to process your details. Third, academic support. The university runs free workshops on essay writing, referencing, and note-taking. These take place on Tuesday and Thursday afternoons in the Learning Resource Centre. You don't need to book; just turn up. However, if you'd like a one-to-one session with a writing tutor, you do need to book online, and slots fill up quickly, so I'd advise doing that early. Fourth, part-time work. Most students on a student visa are permitted to work up to twenty hours per week during term time and full time during holidays. But please check the conditions on your visa, as they can vary. The Careers Service can help you find suitable vacancies, and they also run a workshop on writing a British-style CV. Finally, a word about the social programme. Every Friday evening there's a international café in the Student Union, where you can meet students from all over the world. It's free, and refreshments are provided. Next week, there's also a guided tour of the city centre on Saturday morning, leaving from the main entrance at ten o'clock. Sign-up sheets are by the door. If you have any questions, my office is room 214 in the Hartley Building, and my email address is on the handout you received. Enjoy your studies, and please do come and say hello.","questions":"1. Where can students collect a missing student ID card?\n2. What two documents are needed to collect the student ID card?\n3. What is the nearest doctor's surgery to the campus?\n4. On which days do academic writing workshops take place?\n5. What must students do to get a one-to-one writing tutorial?\n6. How many hours per week can most student visa holders work during term time?\n7. What is provided at the Friday evening international café?\n8. Where does the guided city tour leave from?","answers":"1. The Student Services desk on the ground floor of the Hartley Building. (The speaker says to visit the Student Services desk there.)\n2. A passport and an offer letter. (Both are explicitly required.)\n3. The Mill Road Practice. (Described as the nearest surgery, a ten-minute walk from the main gate.)\n4. Tuesday and Thursday afternoons. (Workshops take place on Tuesday and Thursday afternoons in the Learning Resource Centre.)\n5. Book online. (One-to-one sessions require online booking; slots fill up quickly.)\n6. Up to twenty hours per week. (Most student visa holders may work up to twenty hours during term time.)\n7. Free refreshments. (The café is free and refreshments are provided.)\n8. The main entrance. (The tour leaves from the main entrance at ten o'clock.)","audio":"audio/listening-9.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-7","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":10,"title":"Welcome to Northfield University: Library and Study Support Orientation","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Northfield University. My name is Eleanor Whitcombe, and I'm the head of Library and Learning Services. This short talk will introduce you to the support available in your first term, so please listen carefully, as you'll need this information for your first assignments. The main library, the Hartley Building, is open twenty-four hours a day during term time, but staffed help is available only from eight in the morning until eight in the evening on weekdays, and from ten until four at weekends. Outside those hours, you can still enter with your student card and use the self-service machines. Now, a very common problem for new students is finding the right reading for a module. Each module has a reading list on the virtual learning environment, and items marked 'core' are held in the Short Loan Collection on the ground floor. These can be borrowed for four hours at a time, and you may renew them twice if no one else has reserved them. Standard loans, by contrast, last for three weeks. If a book you need is already on loan, you can place a reservation online; we'll email you when it's ready, and you'll have three days to collect it. Next, I'd like to mention our academic skills workshops. These run every Wednesday afternoon in Room 2.14 on the second floor. Topics include referencing, avoiding plagiarism, and planning essays. They're free, but you must book a place through the student portal, as rooms are small and fill up quickly. For one-to-one help, you can book a twenty-minute appointment with a subject librarian; these are particularly useful before a big project. Finally, a word about the study spaces. The silent study zone is on the fourth floor, and the group study rooms on the third floor can be booked for up to two hours per group per day. There's also a café on the ground floor, which stays open until six. If you have any questions after this talk, please email the library help desk or visit the enquiry point just inside the main entrance. Thank you for listening, and I wish you every success in your studies here at Northfield.","questions":"1. What are the weekday staffed opening hours of the main library?\n2. How long can items from the Short Loan Collection normally be borrowed?\n3. How many times may Short Loan items be renewed if no one else has reserved them?\n4. How long can standard loans be kept?\n5. When a reserved book arrives, how many days do students have to collect it?\n6. On which day and in which room do academic skills workshops take place?\n7. How long is a one-to-one appointment with a subject librarian?\n8. Where is the silent study zone located?","answers":"1. From 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. on weekdays (the transcript states staffed help is available from eight in the morning until eight in the evening on weekdays).\n2. Four hours at a time (items marked 'core' in the Short Loan Collection can be borrowed for four hours).\n3. Twice (students may renew them twice if no one else has reserved them).\n4. Three weeks (standard loans last for three weeks).\n5. Three days (students have three days to collect a reserved book after being emailed).\n6. Wednesday afternoon in Room 2.14 on the second floor (workshops run every Wednesday afternoon in Room 2.14).\n7. Twenty minutes (one-to-one appointments with a subject librarian are twenty minutes long).\n8. On the fourth floor (the silent study zone is on the fourth floor; group study rooms are on the third floor).","audio":"audio/listening-10.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-8","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":11,"title":"University Welcome and Campus Orientation","prompt":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Hartwell University. My name is Dr. Eleanor Hughes, and I'm the Director of Student Services. I'm delighted to see so many new faces here today. This orientation session is designed to help you settle in and make the most of your time with us. Let me begin with a few practical points. Firstly, your student ID cards are essential. You'll need them to access the library, the sports centre, and the computer labs. If you haven't collected yours yet, please go to the Student Services Office on the ground floor of the Main Building. It's open from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Monday to Friday. Secondly, registration for modules closes on Friday, the 15th of September. You must complete your module choices online through the student portal by 5 p.m. on that day. Late registration will incur a fee of fifty pounds, so please don't delay. Now, about the campus facilities. The library is open 24 hours during term time, but from Monday to Friday, the help desk is staffed from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., and at weekends from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. The sports centre offers a range of activities, from swimming to yoga, and membership is included in your tuition fees. However, you need to book sessions in advance using the app. For those interested in joining societies, we have over 150 to choose from. The Freshers' Fair will take place next Wednesday in the Student Union Building from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. It's a great opportunity to meet people and sign up. Finally, a word about academic support. The Writing Centre provides one-to-one tutorials to help with essays and reports. You can book a 30-minute session online. Also, each of you will be assigned a personal tutor. You'll receive an email with their contact details by the end of this week. If you have any questions, feel free to ask me now or drop by the Student Services Office. Thank you, and enjoy your time at Hartwell.","questions":"1. Where can students collect their student ID cards?\n2. What is the deadline for module registration?\n3. What is the penalty for late module registration?\n4. During term time, what are the library's opening hours?\n5. When is the library help desk staffed at weekends?\n6. How can students book sports centre sessions?\n7. When and where is the Freshers' Fair?\n8. How long is a session at the Writing Centre?","answers":"1. At the Student Services Office on the ground floor of the Main Building. (The speaker says: 'please go to the Student Services Office on the ground floor of the Main Building.') 2. Friday, the 15th of September, by 5 p.m. (The speaker says: 'registration for modules closes on Friday, the 15th of September... by 5 p.m. on that day.') 3. A fee of fifty pounds. (The speaker says: 'Late registration will incur a fee of fifty pounds.') 4. The library is open 24 hours during term time. (The speaker says: 'The library is open 24 hours during term time.') 5. From 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. (The speaker says: 'at weekends from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.') 6. By using the app. (The speaker says: 'you need to book sessions in advance using the app.') 7. Next Wednesday in the Student Union Building from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (The speaker says: 'The Freshers' Fair will take place next Wednesday in the Student Union Building from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.') 8. 30 minutes. (The speaker says: 'You can book a 30-minute session online.')","audio":"audio/listening-11.mp3"},{"id":"ext-l-9","type":"listening","skill":"原创听力 · 8题","long":true,"audioIndex":12,"title":"Welcome to Hartwell University: A Campus Orientation Briefing","prompt":"Good morning, and a very warm welcome to Hartwell University. My name is Eleanor Vance, and I'm the head of student services here. I'd like to give you a brief overview of what to expect during your first week, and to point out a few essential things you'll need to know. First, let's talk about registration. All new students must complete their module registration online by this Friday at five p.m. You'll find the link in your welcome email. If you have any trouble, please visit the student help desk on the ground floor of the Ashworth Building. They're open from nine to four, Monday to Friday. Now, a word about your student ID cards. These are crucial. You'll need them to access the library, the sports centre, and even the cafeteria. You can collect your card from the reception desk in the main hall. Please bring your passport or a driving licence as proof of identity. Without an ID card, you won't be able to borrow books or enter examination halls. Next, let me mention the orientation events. There's a campus tour every hour from ten until three today and tomorrow. The tours start from the fountain outside the main entrance. Also, don't miss the welcome fair on Wednesday from eleven to two in the grand hall. Over thirty student societies will be there, and you can sign up for clubs, sports, and volunteering opportunities. It's a great way to make friends. Regarding your timetable, your first lectures begin next Monday. You can view your personal timetable on the student portal. If you have any clashes, contact your department's academic office immediately. Finally, a quick note on health and safety. The university health centre is located on West Road, next to the chemistry labs. You can register with a doctor there. In an emergency, dial 2222 from any campus phone. Well, that's all for now. Enjoy your orientation, and do ask if you have any questions. Thank you.","questions":"1. Who is giving the orientation briefing?\n2. By what day and time must new students complete their online module registration?\n3. Where is the student help desk located?\n4. What two forms of identification are accepted for collecting a student ID card?\n5. What time do the campus tours start each hour?\n6. Where do the campus tours begin?\n7. How many student societies will be at the welcome fair?\n8. What number should you dial from a campus phone in an emergency?","answers":"1. Eleanor Vance, the head of student services.\n2. By Friday at 5 p.m.\n3. On the ground floor of the Ashworth Building.\n4. A passport or a driving licence.\n5. From ten until three (10 a.m. to 3 p.m.).\n6. From the fountain outside the main entrance.\n7. Over thirty (more than 30).\n8. 2222.","audio":"audio/listening-12.mp3"}]};